1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,396
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 18.

2
00:00:05,016 --> 00:00:08,275
Part a: input n, where n is at most one hundred.

3
00:00:08,275 --> 00:00:13,189
If it is out of range, print an error and ask again, until a valid n is entered.

4
00:00:13,189 --> 00:00:14,637
Which loop does this?

5
00:00:16,337 --> 00:00:19,207
Validation needs a loop that runs at least once,

6
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:24,008
and repeats while the input is still invalid. That is exactly a do while.

7
00:00:24,629 --> 00:00:28,923
Check it: the body always reads n first, the error prints only for bad

8
00:00:28,923 --> 00:00:32,996
input, and the condition repeats only while n is still too big.

9
00:00:33,616 --> 00:00:37,996
An if gives only one retry, so a second bad value slips through.

10
00:00:37,996 --> 00:00:41,091
A plain while tests n before it was ever read.

11
00:00:41,091 --> 00:00:44,478
And the last option repeats on valid input instead.

12
00:00:46,078 --> 00:00:51,020
The theory: a while loop tests first, so its body may run zero times.

13
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:55,610
A do while tests at the end, so its body always runs at least once.

14
00:00:55,610 --> 00:01:00,140
Input validation always needs at least one read, so do while fits.

15
00:01:01,739 --> 00:01:05,886
So the answer is the do while loop that reads n, prints the error when n

16
00:01:05,886 --> 00:01:09,815
is over one hundred, and repeats while n is over one hundred.

17
00:01:10,635 --> 00:01:16,064
Using an if, which allows one retry, where a loop with unlimited retries is needed.

18
00:01:16,784 --> 00:01:21,317
Exam tip: the condition of a validation loop describes the bad input.

19
00:01:21,317 --> 00:01:24,603
Read it as: keep asking while the input is bad.
